## Errors

A call that fails gives one of these codes and a message, the same in every language. Each
language raises it in its own way:

| Language | A failure is |
|---|---|
| Rust | `teigeodata_api::Error`, with `code` (an `ErrorCode`) and `message` |
| Python | `TeiGeodataError`, or its subclass for the code: {% for e in error_codes %}`{{ e.name }}Error`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} |
| JavaScript | an `Error` whose `code` is the code's name, such as `"{{ error_codes[0].name }}"` |
| Swift | a thrown `TeiGeodataError`, with `code` (`TeiGeodataError.Code`) |
| Java and Kotlin | `TeiGeodataException` (unchecked), or its subclass for the code, such as `TeiGeodataException.{{ error_codes[1].name }}`; `code()` gives the value |
| Dart | the sealed `TeiGeodataException`: {% for e in error_codes %}`{{ e.name }}Exception`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} |
| Go | an `error` holding a `*teigeodata.Error`; test it with `errors.Is(err, teigeodata.Code{{ error_codes[0].name }})` |
| C | the code as the return value (0 is success); `tg_last_error` gives the message on the same thread |
| C++ | `teigeodata::Error` (a `std::runtime_error`), with `code()` |
| .NET | `TeiGeodataException`, with `Code` (an `ErrorCode`) |

| Code | Value | Meaning |
|---|--:|---|
